# Target C-II — a universal common-host endpoint-order reduction

**Status:** proved finite combinatorial candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.
**Scope:** the collapsed support-six subcase in which all six parallel outer-host
trains terminate at one common outer host.
**Certificate:** `verify_target_c_collapsed_common_host_triangle_orders_2026_09_18.py`.

This reduction is independent of the metric collapsed root.  It uses only the
audited endpoint-fan rule and the audited triangle-free train-crossing rule.

## Statement

The internal train graph is

\[
K_6\setminus\{03,14,25\}.
\]

For a cyclic order of the six bounded endpoints on one common outer host, join two
internal trains when their endpoint chords alternate.  Such a pair must cross in
any common-host embedding.  Exact enumeration of all `6! = 720` labelled endpoint
orders gives:

\[
\boxed{384\text{ orders contain a triangle in this forced-crossing graph}.}
\]

The triangle-free train-crossing theorem therefore excludes those 384 orders for
every collapsed metric realization.  Only 336 endpoint orders remain for any future
common-host attack.  Their forced-crossing graphs have exactly three
degree/bipartiteness types:

| type | count | bipartite? | nonzero degree multiset |
|---|---:|:---:|---|
| A | 48 | yes | `2^6` |
| B | 144 | yes | `2^4 3^2` |
| C | 144 | no | `2^2 3^6` |

Thus the remaining metric problem is finite at the crossing-graph level, but it is
not eliminated by direction-only graph coloring: type A is a 6-cycle and type C
contains odd cycles without a forced triangle.

The proof is exact integer combinatorics; it does not use floating-point angles,
the local closure equations, or a selected numerical chamber.

Dependency chain:

```text
common outer host
  -> cyclic endpoint order
  -> alternating endpoint chords must cross
  -> exact triangle in the forced-crossing graph
  -> contradiction with triangle-free train crossings.
```

This does not exclude the remaining 336 orders, any multi-host attachment, or the
collapsed branch globally.  It is a universal partial reduction, not a C-II closure.

Run:

```text
python3 research/verify_target_c_collapsed_common_host_triangle_orders_2026_09_18.py
```
